"""Focused network procedures and reading-inference examples."""
from . import T

T('9-layers-switching','circuit-session','Circuit switching','Circuit switching: reservation, setup and transfer',r'''
Circuit switching establishes a path and reserves the stated transmission resources before a session sends its data. A reserved rate is a capacity allocation, not the number of useful bits the sender must transmit continuously. An idle session can retain its reservation, preventing another session from using that capacity under the supplied model. This distinguishes resource reservation from a virtual circuit that merely installs packet-forwarding state.
For a numerical problem, separate setup, data serialization and propagation. If setup completes before transfer starts, its delay adds to the transfer time. Serialization of L bits at reserved rate R takes L/R seconds. Add propagation only when given; do not invent packet headers or store-and-forward delays for a circuit model that excludes them.
For simultaneous reservations on a link, compare the sum of reserved rates with link capacity. A session's average useful rate can be smaller than its reservation because it pauses. Verify time units and capacity independently: fitting the data in a time interval does not establish that an additional reservation is available.
''','A 10 Mbps circuit needs 40 ms setup, then sends 1,000,000 bits. Ignore propagation. Its link has capacity 30 Mbps and two other 10 Mbps reservations.',r'Convert the data time::T_{data}=10^6/(10\times10^6)=0.1\ s::The reserved circuit rate determines serialization.~Include setup::T=40+100=140\ ms::Setup precedes the 100 ms data transfer.~Check reservations::R_{reserved}=10+10+10=30\ Mbps::The three sessions occupy all modeled reservation capacity; another 10 Mbps circuit does not fit.', 'Transfer finishes after 140 ms; no fourth 10 Mbps reservation fits.','The data uses its full reserved rate for 100 ms, while session setup accounts for the remaining 40 ms.')

T('9-layers-switching','packet-pipeline','Packet switching','Packet switching: sharing links and overlapping packets',r'''
Packet switching divides a message into independently transmitted packets that share link capacity with other traffic. A store-and-forward router receives a complete packet before sending that packet onward. It can receive a later packet on one link while transmitting an earlier packet on a different link, provided the model supplies independent interfaces. This overlap is the source of packet pipelining.
Draw one timeline for each link. The first packet pays serialization on every store-and-forward link, while later packets can follow at the bottleneck's packet interval. For N equal packets across k equal-rate links, with no other delays or contention, completion takes (N+k−1)L/R. This expression assumes packet length L includes all modeled transmitted bits.
Packet switching does not promise an exclusive rate for each application. Queues, headers, unequal rates and other traffic change this simple timeline and must be included when specified. Compare an actual sequence of packet completions instead of multiplying a single packet's end-to-end time by N, which would incorrectly discard permitted overlap.
''','Split 1,000,000 bits into four equal packets over two 10 Mbps store-and-forward links. Ignore headers, propagation and other traffic.',r'Packet transmission time::L=250000\ bits,\quad t=L/R=25\ ms::Every packet occupies either link for 25 ms.~Start the pipeline::::Packet 1 uses link one during 0–25 ms and link two during 25–50 ms; packet 2 can use link one during 25–50 ms.~Finish all packets::T=(4+2-1)25=125\ ms::Packets leave link two at 50, 75, 100 and 125 ms.', 'The message finishes in 125 ms.','Each link serializes four packets for 100 ms, and the second link starts 25 ms after the first.')

T('9-link-layer','bit-stuffing','Data link layer','Framing: protect a delimiter with bit stuffing',r'''
Framing tells a receiver where one link-layer unit ends and the next begins. A special flag can delimit frames, but arbitrary payload data may contain the same bit pattern. Bit stuffing changes the transmitted payload so that the delimiter cannot accidentally appear inside it. It is a framing transformation, separate from error-detection redundancy such as a CRC.
In the specified HDLC-style exercise, flags are 01111110. Between flags, the sender inserts a zero immediately after every run of five consecutive payload ones. The receiver reverses that rule in the frame interior by removing the zero following five ones. Count runs in the actual sequence, and reset the run after a zero. The flags themselves are not subjected to this payload rule.
Compute overhead from the number of inserted bits, then restore the original payload as a check. Applying stuffing after every five bits regardless of their values is incorrect. Removing arbitrary zeros can corrupt valid data. Real framing rules also handle aborts and malformed sequences; this exercise supplies a correctly formed interior and focuses on reversible delimiter protection.
''','With flag 01111110 and a zero inserted after five consecutive payload ones, transmit payload 01111110. Exclude the flags from length calculations.',r'Find the run::::The payload starts with 0, contains six consecutive ones, and ends with 0.~Insert a protective zero::::After the first five ones insert 0; the remaining original one then starts a new run, producing interior 011111010.~Undo and count::L_{wire}=8+1=9\ bits::Removing the stuffed zero after five ones restores all eight original payload bits.', 'Stuffed frame interior 011111010; one bit of framing overhead.','The interior no longer contains six consecutive ones, and destuffing recovers the original delimiter-shaped payload.')

T('9-link-layer','collision-timing','Medium Access Control','Shared-medium access: why collision detection needs time',r'''
Medium access control decides how transmitters use a shared channel. In a simplified half-duplex CSMA/CD model, a sender senses activity before transmitting, but a far-away sender can begin before the first signal reaches it. The collision indication then takes time to travel back. Carrier sensing therefore does not guarantee that simultaneous transmissions cannot collide.
To detect a worst-case collision while still transmitting, the frame's transmission duration must cover the modeled round-trip propagation time. With one-way propagation delay τ and bit rate R, require L/R≥2τ, giving L≥2τR bits. Account for other delay components only if the question includes them. The result is a timing lower bound in the stated model, not a replacement for Ethernet's protocol-specified minimum frame size.
Keep full-duplex switched links distinct from this shared-channel exercise: classic collision detection does not apply there. Convert microseconds to seconds and bits to bytes carefully. Testing a proposed frame length against the duration inequality gives a direct interpretation of the result rather than treating the expression as a memorized formula.
''','A hypothetical shared half-duplex bus runs at 10 Mbps with maximum one-way propagation 20 μs. Ignore other delays. Find the timing lower bound on frame length and test a 256-bit frame.',r'Round-trip time::2\tau=40\ \mu s::A remote collision can need an outward and return propagation leg.~Minimum bits::L_{min}=10^7(40\times10^{-6})=400\ bits::The transmitter must still be sending for at least 40 microseconds.~Test the shorter frame::256/10^7=25.6\ \mu s<40\ \mu s::That frame can finish before the worst-case collision indication returns.', 'The modeled lower bound is 400 bits, or 50 bytes; 256 bits is too short.','400 bits at 10 Mbps takes exactly 40 μs. This hypothetical timing bound does not change standard Ethernet framing requirements.')

T('9-routing','feedback-trace','Count to infinity','Count to infinity: trace the dependency behind rising metrics',r'''
Count-to-infinity is a distance-vector convergence failure caused by stale routes feeding back through neighboring routers. A numerical advertisement does not describe its whole dependency path. If B's advertised route was originally learned through A, A cannot safely interpret that stale value as an independent replacement after its own direct destination link fails.
For an explicit trace, record the update order, link costs, destination estimates and next hops. Each update adds the local link cost to the neighbor's most recently available advertised distance. Without the specified protections, the two routers can keep replacing their estimates with larger values while sending actual destination traffic toward each other. A finite protocol infinity eventually caps the metric, but does not make the temporary loop a usable route.
Split horizon and poison reverse address this feedback by changing advertisements toward the neighbor used as next hop. They mitigate particular looping patterns rather than establishing universal instantaneous convergence. Show both numerical growth and route dependence: increasing numbers alone do not explain why there is no functioning path to the destination.
''','Initially A reaches D with cost 1 and B reaches D through A with cost 2. A–B costs 1. A–D fails; A uses B’s stale advertisement before B hears the failure. Then A and B alternately update, with no split horizon or poison reverse.',r'First stale replacement::d_A(D)=1+2=3::A chooses B, but B’s advertised route still depends on A.~Next neighbor update::d_B(D)=1+3=4::B keeps A as next hop and adopts A’s new estimate.~Continue feedback::d_A(D)=5,\quad d_B(D)=6::Each alternate update adds one while the next-hop cycle A→B→A remains.', 'The estimates grow 3,4,5,6,… despite the missing route; packets loop between A and B.','At every step the supposed route to D returns to the other router; no independent destination path was created.')

T('9-sockets','bind-endpoint','bind','bind: choose the local socket endpoint',r'''
The bind operation assigns a socket a local address and port under the operating system's rules. A server commonly binds the service port that clients know before entering a listening state. Binding selects the local endpoint; it does not select a remote client, establish a TCP connection or consume the application's first message.
An explicit local interface address restricts the socket to that address in this exercise. A wildcard bind can allow the service on multiple local interfaces, subject to the platform and network configuration. Port zero asks the system to select an available local port; it does not advertise a well-known service port automatically. The application must discover the chosen endpoint when needed.
Check addresses and existing bindings before interpreting a failure. Two ordinary listeners cannot necessarily reserve the same local address/port with default options; address-reuse facilities require stated semantics. Distinguish the bound socket from connected sockets created later by accept. Their endpoint roles differ even when accepted connections share the listener's local service port.
''','A host has addresses 10.0.0.5 and 192.168.1.5. A TCP server binds specifically to 10.0.0.5:8080, then listens. Assume no forwarding or address-reuse options. Which destination endpoint reaches this listener?',r'Identify the bound address::::The chosen local address is 10.0.0.5 and the chosen port is 8080.~Test the matching endpoint::::A connection addressed to 10.0.0.5:8080 matches the local binding.~Test the other interface::::192.168.1.5:8080 has the same port but a different local address, so this specific-address listener does not match it.', 'The listener matches 10.0.0.5:8080, not 192.168.1.5:8080 under these assumptions.','A wildcard address would change the address-matching assumption; using the same port alone is insufficient.')

T('9-sockets','connect-endpoint','connect','connect: establish a TCP endpoint pair',r'''
For TCP, connect requests a connection from a client socket to a specified remote address and port. If the client has not explicitly bound a local endpoint, the operating system ordinarily selects an appropriate local address and an available ephemeral port. The resulting connection is identified by its local and remote endpoints under the transport protocol.
In the supplied blocking, successful model, TCP's establishment exchange completes before connect returns success. That does not mean the server application has read a request. A listener can queue an established connection before the application calls accept. Accept gives the server application a connected socket for that endpoint pair; the listener remains available for further connections.
Do not confuse the server's destination service port with the client's chosen source port. Multiple clients can connect to one server port because their endpoint pairs differ. Errors, nonblocking completion and timeouts need their stated API rules. Trace connection establishment separately from application sends and receives, since an established transport is a channel rather than evidence that an HTTP or other request was processed.
''','Client 10.0.0.8 uses local TCP port 51000 and successfully connects to 10.0.0.5:8080. The server listener later accepts it. No application data has yet been sent.',r'Client endpoint pair::::The client socket is local 10.0.0.8:51000 and remote 10.0.0.5:8080.~Server endpoint pair::::The accepted socket reverses perspective: local 10.0.0.5:8080 and remote 10.0.0.8:51000.~Separate channel and message::::Connection success establishes TCP state, but without a send there is no application request to process.', 'The client and accepted server sockets describe the same TCP connection from opposite perspectives.','The server’s listener stays on port 8080; accept does not require assigning a new local service port for each client.')

T('9-dns','record-chain','Resource records','Resource records: follow aliases and expire each record separately',r'''
A DNS resource record gives a name typed data rather than an undifferentiated answer string. An A record contains an IPv4 address; a CNAME record names an alias target. Resolving an alias for an IPv4 address means following the name and finding the target's A record, possibly through additional queries. The alias target itself is not a numeric network endpoint.
Cache lifetime belongs to each stored record. A CNAME and its target address can have different TTLs, and reading either record does not restart its lifetime. When one expires, the resolver can reuse other still-valid records under their applicable rules and obtain fresh information for the expired part. The entire chain need not be discarded simply because its shortest-lived record expires.
Trace names, record types, ages and remaining TTLs separately. A cached alias can survive an address change at its target, so refreshing the A record can discover a new address without changing the CNAME. This exercise assumes ordinary expiry, no eviction and no refresh before the queried time; advanced negative caching or policy changes require additional facts.
''','At t=0 a resolver caches www.example.test CNAME edge.example.test with TTL 300 s, and edge.example.test A 203.0.113.7 with TTL 60 s. At t=90 it receives another IPv4 lookup for www.example.test. No refresh or eviction occurred.',r'Follow the name::::The CNAME identifies edge.example.test as the name whose A record is needed.~Check the alias lifetime::300-90=210\ s::The CNAME still has 210 seconds of validity.~Check the address lifetime::60-90<0::The old A record has expired; obtain a fresh A record for edge.example.test while reusing the valid alias.', 'Reuse the CNAME; refresh the target’s expired A record before answering with an address.','The two original records expired at t=300 and t=60 respectively, confirming that at t=90 only the address record is stale.')

T('9-http','method-retry','Methods','HTTP methods: reason about the effect of a retry',r'''
An HTTP method describes the intended action on the target resource. GET requests retrieval; PUT supplies a representation to create or replace a specifically identified resource; POST submits content for processing under the resource's contract. Method semantics help a client reason about retries, but the application's endpoint behavior must also be read.
Idempotence means that repeating the same request has the same intended resource effect as applying it once. It does not require identical status codes, timestamps or response bytes. A first PUT might create a resource and a later identical PUT update it while leaving the same final representation. A POST that creates a fresh job for every processing event can create two jobs when a request is retried without deduplication.
When a response is lost, the client may be uncertain whether the server processed the request. Trace the original operation and retry separately, then compare final state. An idempotency key can change a POST endpoint's retry contract, but do not assume one when the question explicitly excludes deduplication. Safe methods and idempotent methods are different classifications.
''','The API stores exactly the supplied settings for PUT /settings. Every POST /jobs creates a new job, with no idempotency key. Each of these requests is processed twice because the client retries a lost response: PUT /settings with theme=dark; POST /jobs with task=backup.',r'First PUT::::The settings resource becomes theme=dark.~Repeated PUT::::Applying the identical replacement again leaves that same settings value; there is still one settings resource.~Both POST operations::::The first processing creates one backup job and the retry creates another under the specified endpoint contract.', 'Final settings are theme=dark; two backup jobs exist.','The retry preserves PUT’s intended final state while the supplied POST contract performs the creation twice; response equality is irrelevant.')

T('9-http','cache-validation','Caching','HTTP caching: freshness, validators and a 304 response',r'''
An HTTP cache stores a representation together with the response metadata needed to decide whether it can be reused. Freshness permits reuse without contacting the origin under the supplied cache policy. Once stale, a stored response may still be useful if the cache validates it rather than downloading its entire body again.
An entity tag is a validator identifying a representation version. A conditional GET with If-None-Match asks the origin to send the representation only when its current tag differs. A matching validator can produce 304 Not Modified, allowing the cache to retain its stored body while updating permitted metadata. A 304 therefore saves representation transfer but still involves a request/response exchange. A changed representation normally produces 200 with its new body and tag.
Read the freshness rules and timing assumptions explicitly. Cache-Control directives, authorization and invalidation can restrict reuse; this example supplies a reusable response with max-age and no such extra restrictions. Track the stored body's identity separately from age: fresh reuse, stale validation and replacement are three different outcomes.
''','At t=0 a cache stores a reusable 200 response with body B1, ETag "v1", and max-age=60 s. Requests arrive at t=30 and t=80. The origin still has v1 at t=80 and responds to validation with fresh metadata. At t=150 the cached response is stale again and the origin now has v2.',r'Fresh request::age=30<60::At t=30 the cache returns B1 without an origin exchange.~Validate the stale request::age=80>60::At t=80 send If-None-Match: "v1" instead of assuming the old body is still current.~Unchanged origin::::The origin returns 304; retain body B1 and update the supplied freshness metadata.~Changed origin::::At t=150 the conditional request’s v1 differs from current v2, so receive 200 with body B2 and replace the stored version.', 't=30: reuse B1; t=80: validate with 304 and retain B1; t=150: receive 200 and replace it with B2.','A 304 contains no replacement representation body; it confirms reuse of the previously stored body under the response’s validation semantics.')

T('9-tcp-web','congestion-loss','TCP congestion control','TCP congestion control: threshold growth and two loss responses',r'''
TCP congestion control uses a congestion window cwnd to limit outstanding data according to perceived network conditions. It is separate from the receiver's advertised window rwnd; the smaller applicable window can limit transmission. Simplified RTT exercises must specify their growth and loss rules because actual TCP implementations and acknowledgment patterns vary.
In this supplied Reno-style model, reaching the slow-start threshold ssthresh changes growth to one MSS per complete loss-free RTT. On three duplicate acknowledgments, set ssthresh to half the current window, enter fast recovery at ssthresh plus three MSS, and return cwnd to ssthresh when the specified new-data acknowledgment ends recovery. A timeout instead resets cwnd to one MSS while reducing ssthresh. These are the stated exercise rules, not a universal description of every congestion controller.
Record cwnd and ssthresh separately at each event. A loss reaction does not mean the receiver ran out of buffer space, and the temporary recovery window is not the later congestion-avoidance window. Compare alternative loss events from the same pre-loss state so their different consequences are visible.
''','Use this simplified Reno-style model: cwnd=8 MSS and ssthresh=8 MSS; each complete loss-free RTT in congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS. After two RTTs, compare three duplicate ACKs with a timeout. Both set ssthresh=cwnd/2; duplicate ACKs set recovery cwnd=ssthresh+3 then cwnd=ssthresh on the new-data ACK; timeout sets cwnd=1.',r'Apply congestion avoidance::cwnd:8\to9\to10\ MSS::The initial window is at the threshold, so the supplied additive rule applies.~Reduce the threshold::ssthresh=10/2=5\ MSS::Both alternative loss events start from the same ten-MSS window.~Duplicate-ACK recovery::cwnd=5+3=8\ MSS\to5\ MSS::Eight is the temporary recovery window; the specified acknowledgment ends recovery at five.~Timeout alternative::cwnd=1\ MSS,\quad ssthresh=5\ MSS::A timeout restarts with the stated small congestion window instead.', 'After two RTTs cwnd=10 MSS. Duplicate ACKs give ssthresh=5 and recovery cwnd 8 then 5; timeout gives ssthresh=5 and cwnd=1.','Both loss paths halve the same pre-loss window, while only the timeout resets cwnd to one MSS under the supplied rules.')

T('9-tcp-web','local-socket-workflow','Socket API','Socket API in this lesson: a client and server exchange bytes',r'''
A socket API exposes transport communication through operating-system objects. For a TCP server, socket creates an endpoint object, bind chooses the local service endpoint, listen prepares a connection queue, and accept returns a connected socket for a particular client. The listener remains available; an accepted socket is the channel used for that client's byte exchange.
A client creates its own socket and uses connect to request the server endpoint. Successful connection establishment precedes the application's request exchange in this simplified blocking model. Send and recv transfer byte counts, so callers must account for partial progress. TCP preserves ordered bytes but does not promise that one send is delivered by exactly one recv.
Trace listener and connected sockets as different objects. Match the client and server endpoint pairs, then count bytes still required by the application protocol. Closing a connected socket affects that connection; the listener can continue serving other clients. This local walkthrough introduces the whole workflow, while the separate socket lesson gives additional endpoint and framing examples.
''','A server binds 10.0.0.5:8080 and listens. Client 10.0.0.8:51000 connects; the server accepts. The application expects a fixed six-byte request, but its first recv returns two bytes.',r'Prepare the listener::::bind selects 10.0.0.5:8080 and listen makes it available for incoming connections.~Accept the endpoint pair::::accept returns a connected socket with remote endpoint 10.0.0.8:51000; the listening socket remains available.~Complete the request::6-2=4\ bytes::Preserve the first two bytes and continue receiving four more before processing the complete fixed-size request.', 'Use the accepted socket for this client and accumulate four additional request bytes.','Two received bytes plus four remaining bytes equals the protocol’s six-byte request; connect/accept alone did not deliver that request.')

T('9-tcp-web','local-dns-workflow','DNS','DNS in this lesson: resolve a service name before connecting',r'''
DNS provides typed information about a domain name; an A lookup asks for an IPv4 address. A client stub usually delegates resolution to a recursive resolver. On a cache miss, that resolver can follow referrals through the hierarchy to an authoritative server. The returned address identifies a potential remote host, while the application's protocol separately supplies its service port.
For delay questions, determine the cache state and which exchanges occur serially. This exercise measures time after the recursive resolver receives the client's request and omits the stub-to-resolver exchange. With no cached delegation, three serial resolver exchanges require the sum of their RTTs. Valid cached results can avoid those exchanges on a later lookup until their supplied lifetime expires.
Resolution does not establish a TCP connection or complete an HTTP request. Treat naming, connection setup and application exchange as separate timeline stages. Root and top-level servers can supply referrals rather than the requested final address. Keep the requested record type explicit: a server name in an NS or MX record is not itself an IPv4 address.
''','After receiving a client’s query, an empty recursive resolver makes one serial exchange each with root, TLD and authoritative servers. Each exchange takes 20 ms; no processing time is counted. The final A record is 203.0.113.9 with TTL 60 s. A repeat query arrives 10 s later.',r'Follow referrals::::Root identifies the relevant TLD service, which identifies the authoritative service; the final server supplies the A record.~Sum the resolver time::T=20+20+20=60\ ms::The three exchanges are serial under the stated empty-cache model.~Check later reuse::TTL_{remaining}=60-10=50\ s::With no refresh or eviction, the repeat can use the cached address and avoid these upstream exchanges.', 'The initial upstream work takes 60 ms; the repeat has 50 seconds of remaining record validity.','The 60 ms excludes the explicitly omitted client-resolver exchange and any later TCP or HTTP work.')

T('9-tcp-web','local-http-workflow','HTTP','HTTP in this lesson: separate request, response and connection time',r'''
HTTP exchanges application requests and responses. A request identifies a method and target; a response carries a status and associated headers and representation when applicable. A successful TCP connection only supplies the transport channel. The application must still send its request and interpret the resulting HTTP response.
For a timing exercise, distinguish connection setup from the request/response exchange. In the stated simplified HTTP-over-TCP model, a new connection costs one RTT to establish, then a tiny request and response cost one further RTT. Ignore DNS, TLS, transmission and processing only because the prompt excludes them. An already established persistent connection removes that first setup cost, not the remaining application exchange.
A 200 response means the request succeeded under HTTP semantics; a 404 is also an HTTP response but indicates the target was not found. Neither status should be confused with a connection failing before any response arrives. State protocol/version assumptions because HTTP/3 and different setup or parallelism models do not share every TCP timing rule.
''','An already resolved server is accessed using HTTP over a new TCP connection. RTT is 40 ms. Count one RTT for TCP setup and one RTT for a tiny GET /guide and its 200 response. Ignore TLS, transfer and processing. Compare using a connection already established.',r'New TCP setup::T_{setup}=40\ ms::No HTTP request exchange has completed during this setup stage.~Get the response::T_{HTTP}=40\ ms::The application request and response require the supplied additional RTT.~Compare totals::T_{new}=80\ ms,\quad T_{existing}=40\ ms::An existing connection saves setup but still performs the GET exchange.', '80 ms with new TCP setup; 40 ms with the existing connection.','Both cases include one application exchange, and only the new-connection case includes the extra setup RTT.')

T('10-verbal-reasoning','passage-inference','Verbal aptitude','Passage inference: preserve the strength of the evidence',r'''
A passage-inference question asks what its statements justify, rather than which option agrees with outside knowledge. Identify the population, conditions and quantifiers before selecting a conclusion. “Some” establishes at least one instance, “all” covers every instance, and “only” imposes a restriction. Changing those words can make a plausible conclusion stronger than the passage permits.
Separate observations from causal explanations. A report that participants' scores improved does not by itself establish that an intervention caused the improvement or that everyone using it will improve. Such claims may need a comparison group, controlled conditions or additional evidence. A necessary inference follows from the supplied statements; a merely possible explanation need not.
For each option, locate the exact supporting clause or exhibit a situation consistent with the passage in which the option is false. Preserve qualifiers such as “among the volunteers” and “during the measured period.” This method is useful even when the subject matter is unfamiliar, because the inference depends on the stated evidence rather than personal expectations.
''','Passage: “In a study of 40 volunteers using a practice app, 26 improved their test scores after four weeks. The study included no comparison group.” Which follows: A) every app user improves; B) some study volunteers improved; C) the app alone caused every improvement?',r'Extract the observation::26\text{ of }40\text{ improved}::The passage asserts improvement for a nonempty subset of the measured volunteers.~Check the supported option::::B preserves that population and makes the weaker claim that some improved, so it follows.~Reject stronger claims::::A extends the observation to every user; C introduces exclusive causation without a comparison or other causal evidence.', 'B follows; A and C are unsupported by the passage.','A study where the remaining fourteen volunteers did not improve and another factor helped the twenty-six still fits the passage, refuting the stronger deductions.')
